# Mobile MVC

Apply when editing files under `mobile/`. Use MVC inside the mobile folder. Prefer existing patterns over inventing new structure or state/networking stacks.

## Structure

```text
mobile/
├── models/          # data shapes, local entities, mappers
├── views/           # screens, widgets, UI only
├── controllers/     # UI orchestration, input handling, navigation triggers
├── services/        # API / device / storage access
├── navigation/      # routes and navigators (if present)
└── utils/           # pure helpers
```

Adapt names to the actual framework (Flutter, React Native, native) if an established layout differs, but keep the MVC separation.

## Layering

- **Models**: data structures, parsing/mapping, local persistence shapes. No UI.
- **Views**: presentation and user interaction widgets/screens only. No API calls.
- **Controllers**: wire views to models/services; handle loading/error flow and navigation intents.
- **Services**: HTTP, device APIs, secure storage — the only place for network calls.

## Conventions

- Reuse existing widgets/components and services.
- Keep API calls in `services/`; never call the network from views.
- Do not duplicate backend business rules on the client.
- Handle offline, loading, empty, success, and error states where applicable.
- Do not introduce a new state-management or networking pattern without justification.
- Keep controllers thin: orchestrate, do not become a second backend.

## Example

```text
// ❌ BAD — view fetches API and embeds business rules
View -> fetch('/users') -> render

// ✅ GOOD — MVC flow
View -> Controller -> Service (API) -> Model -> Controller -> View
```
